{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## DECISION-3B5F-B: REVISED — **re-home, not wire-or-delete**. Symmetry is not a defence.\n\nThe operator's correction: *\"If Cloud and DC both fail to implement functionality necessary for sync\nwith their respective ticket systems, they are both deficient.\"* That is right, and DECISION-3B5F-A\nleaned on symmetry as though it were exculpatory. It is not — it is two deficiencies, not zero. The\nquestion is not \"is this DC-specific?\" but \"is this functionality necessary?\"\n\n### What is genuinely missing, measured\n\nNecessary and PRESENT: the deletion-vs-aged-out discrimination. Two live implementations\n(`outbound_differ._safe_get_issue`, `binding_walk.py:187-196`), and verified live on real DC —\n`test_a_deleted_dc_issue_never_plans_a_local_teardown` **PASSED** in harness run 30933704255.\n\nMissing: c244's **same-pass** re-creation of a hard-deleted issue whose local ticket still exists.\n\nI claimed in DECISION-3B5F-A that the outcome still happens \"eventually\" and did not test it. Tested\nnow, against a real `BindingStore`:\n\n    bind_confirm(local-1, DIG-1)      -> get_jira_key = 'DIG-1'  is_bound = True\n    note_absent #1 (confirmed 404)    -> 'DIG-1'\n    note_absent #2                    -> 'DIG-1'\n    note_absent #3                    -> None      <-- retired at grace\n    FINAL                             -> get_jira_key = None, is_bound = False\n\n`_retire` (`binding_store.py:564-577`) pops the entry from BOTH `bindings` and `reverse`, so the local\nticket is genuinely unbound, and `outbound_differ.py:502-513` then emits the outbound CREATE for it.\nSo the claim holds — but it is a **latency** deficiency (3 passes instead of 1), not a lost capability.\n\n### Why that still leaves us deficient, and why DELETE-as-written was the wrong call\n\nThree passes of delay is a real regression against a documented guarantee, and ADR 0028's Consequences\nassert the same-pass behaviour as a CLOSED gap: *\"Confirmed hard-delete of a still-locally-present\nticket now re-creates it (c244) … This closes the former `epic-3e36` gap where the follow-on was built\nbut never consumed.\"* The follow-on is, again, never consumed. **The ADR is currently inaccurate**, and\nthat is true whichever way this ticket goes.\n\nDeleting the chain and accepting the delay would have made the deficiency permanent and undocumented.\nThat is what the correction catches.\n\n### The third option the brief did not enumerate\n\nNeither WIRE (migrate the legacy `(prev, curr)` caller) nor DELETE (drop the chain) is right:\n\n* WIRE re-lights the snapshot differ's `field_drift` and `unbound_local` arms\n  (`reconcile_helpers.py:505-511`), which now duplicate `outbound_differ` (local↔remote diff) and\n  `binding_walk` (drift classes A and B). `binding_walk.py:120-123` states that division of labour\n  explicitly. Re-enabling a legacy arm to recover one property while double-emitting two others is a\n  bad trade.\n* DELETE, per above, makes the deficiency permanent.\n\n**RE-HOME instead: delete the dead legacy arm, and move probe consumption to `binding_walk`, where the\nconfirmed 404 already exists.** Concretely:\n\n1. `binding_walk` already performs the bounded GET and already knows `CONFIRMED_404`. On that state,\n   for a binding whose LOCAL ticket is still present, emit the inbound-delete follow-on so\n   `_apply_inbound_delete` (`apply_inbound.py:175`) runs and `create_after_hard_delete`\n   (`apply_inbound.py:220`) fires **in the same pass** — restoring c244 without touching the differ.\n2. Have `binding_walk` reach the probe through the **backend port** `probe_remote`\n   (`SupportsAbsenceProbe`, `_backend.py:508-517`) rather than reaching into another module's private\n   `outbound_differ._safe_get_issue`. That fixes a real layering violation AND gives the capability a\n   live caller. Both adapters already implement it — Cloud `adapters/jira/backend.py:272`, DC\n   `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:440` → `transport.py:213`, the latter classifying through the\n   shared `classify_probe_response` bound to configured `resolved_statuses`.\n3. Delete the genuinely dead legacy pieces: the producer\n   `differ._compute_mutations_emit_absent_partner_probes`, the dispatch arm\n   `_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch`, `route_inbound_probe`, and the fixture-only\n   `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_differ_emits_probe.py`.\n4. KEEP `inbound_probe.py` (`ProbeBranch`/`ProbeResult`) — it becomes the live vocabulary\n   `probe_remote` returns and `binding_walk` consumes, not dead code.\n5. `drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions` **stays**. The caller is unchanged, so the suppression\n   remains exactly as correct as today. (The ticket's \"what is NOT the fix\" trap is about WIRE; this\n   path does not touch the caller.)\n6. Correct ADR 0028's Consequences to describe where the same-pass re-create actually lives.\n\n### Scope note\n\nThis is materially larger than the deletion I scoped in DECISION-3B5F-A — it touches the hot pass and\nrestores a destructive-adjacent behaviour — so it needs `file_impact` + `review-plan` before\nimplementation, and a RED-first test proving `(inbound, delete)` is produced from the REAL production\npath (AC #2's requirement, now satisfiable because `binding_walk` IS a real producer)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785868767453423001,"uuid":"50603e25-9e35-47bc-8ab0-a6cd836f4555"}